Add bd_open_stream_dev(): application block reader plus a drive for libaacs

bd_open_stream() lets the application supply the blocks, but passes NULL as the
device path, which silently disables AACS: disc_open() forwards that path to
dec_init() as the drive libaacs should talk to, and libaacs needs one to run the
bus authentication. So today a player can either read the disc itself or decrypt
it, not both.

Both is exactly what Mac OS X 10.4 requires. Tiger has no Blu-ray storage family,
so a BD-ROM is published as IOCDMedia with a 2352 byte block size: the volume
never mounts and every read on /dev/[r]diskN returns EOF at any offset. Sending
READ(10) over SCSITaskLib is the only way to get the bytes -- and SCSITaskLib
grants exclusive access to a single owner, so the player that reads the disc is
also the only one that can hold the channel libaacs needs. It hands that channel
over separately (see libaacs' aacs_use_external_mmc); what is missing here is
just a way to keep the device path alongside the callback.

No behaviour changes for existing callers. disc_open() already prefers the
callback for the data when one is given -- udf_image_open() only falls back to
opening the path itself when read_blocks is NULL -- so passing both is
unambiguous.

--- a/src/libbluray/bluray.c
+++ b/src/libbluray/bluray.c
@@ -1545,7 +1545,32 @@
     fs_access fs = { read_blocks_handle, read_blocks, NULL, NULL };
     return _bd_open(bd, NULL, NULL, &fs);
 }
+
+int bd_open_stream_dev(BLURAY *bd,
+                       void *read_blocks_handle,
+                       int (*read_blocks)(void *handle, void *buf, int lba, int num_blocks),
+                       const char *device_path,
+                       const char *keyfile_path)
+{
+    if (!read_blocks) {
+        return 0;
+    }
 
+    /* Same as bd_open_stream(), except the device path is kept. bd_open_stream()
+     * drops it, which silently disables AACS: disc_open() hands it to dec_init()
+     * as the drive libaacs should talk to, and libaacs needs a drive to run the
+     * bus authentication. disc_open() prefers the callback for the data either
+     * way -- udf_image_open() only falls back to opening the path itself when no
+     * read_blocks was given -- so passing both is unambiguous.
+     *
+     * This exists for players that have to read the disc themselves and still
+     * want AACS, which on Mac OS X 10.4 is the only way a Blu-ray can be read at
+     * all: there BD media is misdetected as a CD, so it never mounts and the
+     * block device returns EOF. */
+    fs_access fs = { read_blocks_handle, read_blocks, NULL, NULL };
+    return _bd_open(bd, device_path, keyfile_path, &fs);
+}
+
 int bd_open_files(BLURAY *bd,
                   void *handle,
                   struct bd_dir_s *(*open_dir)(void *handle, const char *rel_path),
